 thought  couldn't be bent his fight for  disability Justice changed perspectives  and changed lives and to those who knew  him he remained kind generous and  genuine the entire way joining us now is  someone who worked with him for a long  time Kathleen Nguyen she served as  Ontario Premier for much of onley's term  thank you so much for joining us what do  you remember the most about David only  I think my um my Everlasting impression  and memory of David only is going to be  his compassion and his determination you  know he was determined to be an activist  for people with disabilities he was  determined to fight for a more inclusive  Society but he was kind and he was  gentle and he was always willing to work  with anyone who was on side you know he  wasn't partisan he just wanted he wanted  to get the work done and that that was  how I knew him when I first met him in  the late 90s and that was how I knew him  when I was premier  I was very fortunate to work with him at  the beginning of my career he was  passionate and driven but incredibly  humble are there any memories that  you're thinking about tonight Ms win  I remember being at a downtown Toronto  school so I was a parent activist it was  in the late 90s and I was fired up about  what was going on in uh in government at  that point and David was a reporter and  I remember being in a school that was  really inaccessible and he was very  agitated you know he was very clear and  he kind of went out of that journalist  neutral character and was talking to us  about how how unjust our built  um environment was and that was my first  that was my first sort of window on that  determination that he he brought to  everything that he did I didn't know him  I knew him as a reporter but but I saw  the man in that moment where he was just  saying you know this is ridiculous can't  get into these schools he shows up at  buildings and he can't get in and um and  that you know that anger I think fueled  the the activist work that he did when  he was advising Dalton McGinty and then  when he was advising me and then when  and when he was lieutenant governor and  he would travel around the province and  he made it clear everywhere he went that  we all had to do more in order to in  include children and adults in our  society  what Legacy does he leave behind for  Ontario and for Canada  I think he leaves a legacy of  um of determined activism and he rose to  the highest office in the province and  he did it with Grace and he showed what  what anyone can do no matter what their  uh no matter what their ability on I  think that I think that was a very proud  thing for him that he was able to travel  in all of these circles from being a  journalist from being a  um an activist to uh to being the  lieutenant governor of the province and  he used that platform and I think that  the The Province and the country have  been changed because of his  determination and his his modeling of  that courage  Kathleen Nguyen thank you so much for  joining us and for sharing your memories  tonight it was an honor thank you  and that's global national for this  Sunday night I'm Farah Nasser there will  no doubt be many tributes ahead for  David only and he'll be sorely missed by  friends in broadcasting and politics  right across Canada  we leave you tonight with the flags at  half mast in Queens Park and City Hall  in Toronto today in honor of Ontario's  late lieutenant governor and champion of  disability rights on behalf of our whole  crew I want to thank you for spending  part of your evening with us Donna we'll  be back with you tomorrow until next  time take care of yourselves and each  other have a good night  [Music]  foreign
 

